# The invariants this plan holds that a grep can hold.
#
# One rule per block, blank line between blocks, four fields each:
#
#   id       the rule name, and also the directory under near-miss/ that holds
#            the file this rule must fire on
#   paths    a grep --include glob deciding which files the rule reads
#   pattern  an extended regular expression; a match anywhere in a scanned file
#            fails the lint
#   why      what failure the rule prevents, and the issue that argues for it
#
# Adding a rule is adding a block here and a file under near-miss/<id>/. The
# self test derives everything else from the id, so a rule without a near miss
# fails the run rather than passing quietly.
#
# This directory is not scanned. A rule file naturally contains the strings its
# rules refuse, and a lint that reads itself refuses itself.

id: no-query-from-the-request
paths: *.cs
pattern: \[From(Query|Route|Body|Form|Header)\][^,)]*\b([Ss]ql|[Qq]uery|[Ww]here|[Oo]rderBy|[Cc]olumns|[Ss]elect|[Gg]roupBy|[Hh]aving)\b
why: An endpoint that takes a query fragment, a sort column or a column list from the caller lets the caller decide what the database does. Every report endpoint here takes named values and nothing else. Issue #55.

id: no-sql-built-by-concatenation
paths: *.cs
pattern: \$?"[^"]*\b(SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|FROM|WHERE|ORDER BY|GROUP BY)\b[^"]*("[[:space:]]*\+|\{)
why: A statement assembled from strings is a statement whose shape depends on its input. Parameters are passed as parameters, always. Issue #55, and the store issues it is built on.

id: no-identifying-network-or-path-detail-in-the-store
paths: *.cs
pattern: (RemoteEndPoint|UserAgent|User-Agent|\b[Ii]tem\.Path\b)
why: A network address, a user agent and a library file path are the fields that turn playback statistics into a record of where somebody was and what their disk holds. They are refused at the source rather than dropped later. Issues #27 and #48.

id: no-name-in-a-log-message
paths: *.cs
pattern: "[^"]*\{[A-Za-z]*(UserName|Username|ItemName|ItemTitle|SeriesName|EpisodeName|AlbumName|ClientName|DeviceName)\}
why: A log is read by more people than the database is, it is copied into bug reports, and it outlives every retention setting this plan can enforce, so the messages carry identifiers and not names. The pattern refuses the placeholder rather than the call, because a message template in this tree sits on a line of its own and a rule reading the call would not see it. That makes the rule stricter than the policy it enforces, which allows a title at debug level, and it names the placeholders rather than every one ending in a name, because a generic type parameter inside a cref ends in one too and that was measured. The near miss is the closest prior art's own start-up block, which writes the item title, the client and the device out at information level, one line each. Issue #48.

id: no-test-that-drives-a-browser
paths: *
pattern: (Selenium|Playwright|WebDriver|ChromeDriver|GeckoDriver|puppeteer)
why: A browser driven by a test needs a display or a downloaded browser and a running server. The page work is kept in modules with no document access and unit tested directly, and the endpoints are tested in process. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-needs-a-display
paths: *
pattern: ([Xx]vfb|DISPLAY=|xdotool|\bXvnc\b)
why: A runner has no screen, and a test that borrows a virtual one has moved the problem rather than removed it. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-asks-for-elevation
paths: *
pattern: (\brunas\b|\bnetsh\b|\bsc\.exe\b|requireAdministrator|Start-Process[^\n]*-Verb)
why: A test that needs administrator changes the machine it runs on, and on the machine somebody is sitting at it raises a consent prompt in front of them. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-touches-a-certificate-store
paths: *
pattern: (dev-certs|X509Store|StoreName\.(Root|My)|\bcertmgr\b|\bcertutil\b)
why: Trusting a certificate is a change to the machine that outlives the run, and it is the change a failing https test most invites. Endpoint tests run in process, over no socket at all. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-outbound-http-client
paths: *.cs
pattern: (HttpClient|IHttpClientFactory|HttpMessageInvoker|SocketsHttpHandler|WebClient|HttpWebRequest|WebRequest\.Create)
why: This plugin reads the server it runs inside and talks to nothing outside it. Proving that by watching the network needs a network namespace and root, which the headless policy refuses, so the client that would make the call is refused instead. That is a stronger statement than one observed quiet run, because it holds for runs nobody watched. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-ambient-clock
paths: *.cs
except: ServerClock.cs
pattern: (DateTime|DateTimeOffset)\.(Now|UtcNow|Today)|TimeZoneInfo\.Local\b|TimeProvider\.System
why: This plugin is about durations and calendar days, and a file that reads the machine clock or the machine zone behaves differently on the runner than on the server, and differently in June than on the last day of December. Every moment arrives as an argument: capture reads the timestamp the server put on the event, and a day is read in a zone that is named at the call. The suite is scanned too, because a test that reads the clock is the same defect with a shorter fuse. The near miss is a retention cutoff, and the repair its comment names is the parameter this rule is about; what that comment also says, that the task framework passes a moment in, is not so. A scheduled task is handed a progress reporter and a cancellation token, and neither supported server line registers a clock in the container it builds a task out of, so one file has to read the machine clock for every other one to take it as an argument. ServerClock.cs is that file and holds nothing else. Issue #23, and issue #32 for the file spared.

id: no-server-value-in-a-page-asset
paths: *.html
pattern: \{\{[^}]*\}\}|<%[^>]*%>|@(Model|ViewBag|ViewData|Html)\b|%%[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*%%
why: The endpoint that returns a plugin page's content carries no authorization attribute and the server configures no fallback policy, so every page asset here is handed to an unauthenticated caller. A marker left for a server, a template engine or a build step to fill in is how a name, a total or an identifier ends up in a file anybody can fetch, and it is put there by whoever writes the page rather than by whoever reviews the endpoint. Values arrive after the page loads, over an authorized request, or they do not arrive. The rule reads the tracked file; PageAssetTests reads what was built out of it and also refuses an identifier that is not the plugin's own. Neither half can tell a value somebody typed in by hand from ordinary page text. Issue #63.

id: no-statement-that-drops-a-table
paths: *.cs
pattern: [Dd][Rr][Oo][Pp][[:space:]]+[Tt][Aa][Bb][Ll][Ee]
why: The closest prior art answers a schema change by dropping the table and recreating it, so a user who upgrades loses their history and hears about it from a log line. Migrations here move data and never discard it. The standard SQLite table rebuild ends in a drop, so a migration that genuinely needs one has to argue this rule out of this file in a diff rather than doing it quietly. Issue #28.

id: no-external-host-in-a-page-asset
paths: *.html
pattern: (src|href)[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*["'](https?:)?//|@import[[:space:]]+(url\()?["']?(https?:)?//|(fetch|importScripts)\([[:space:]]*["'](https?:)?//
why: The closest prior art ships a minified chart library and loads what it does not ship from somebody else's server. A script fetched at page load is code nobody in this repository read, running in a signed-in administrator's dashboard, and it fails outright on a server with no way out to the internet; a stylesheet or a font is the same request and additionally tells its host which server opened the page. Everything a page needs is served from the plugin, and the charts are drawn by a module of this plugin's own rather than by a bundle. The rule reads tracked HTML, which is every page asset in the tree today; a script or stylesheet kept in a file of its own needs its own block here, because a rule carries one path glob. Issue #62.

id: no-configuration-value-in-a-static-field
paths: *.cs
pattern: static[^;=(]*=[^;]*\bConfiguration\b|static[[:space:]]+(readonly[[:space:]]+)?PluginConfiguration\b
why: A setting read once into a static field is a setting the page can no longer change. The administrator who turns capture off watches it go on recording until the next restart, the page saved cleanly, and nothing anywhere says the two disagree. Every consumer takes the configuration through a constructor and reads the current value at the moment it needs one. The rule reads a declaration, so it catches the field that is initialised where it is declared and the field whose type is the configuration itself; a static assigned from inside a method later is not a shape it can see, and no grep can see one. Issue #72.

id: no-store-write-outside-the-write-path
paths: *.cs
except: IPlayStore.cs SqlitePlayStore.cs QueuedPlayWriter.cs PlayArchive.cs RetentionSweep.cs UserDeletedConsumer.cs UnknownUserSweep.cs OwnHistoryDeletion.cs ConsentRegister.cs FinishWhatARestartLeftOpen.cs AggregateQueries.cs
except-in: Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests
pattern: IPlayStore
why: A setting that turns capture off, and a user who asked not to be recorded, are honoured in one function immediately before the write. Anything that reaches the store on its own goes round that function, and what it writes is a row nobody consented to. The rule refuses the store's interface being named at all, which is the greppable form of the property: reaching the store is naming it. The files spared are the interface itself, the store, the queue that is the write path, the archive, whose import is the one other operation whose job is to put rows in, the retention sweep, which is the fifth this rule's earlier wording said would need a line here and an argument in a pull request and took one on issue #32, the consumer that deletes a removed account's rows, which took one on issue #44, the sweep that deletes the rows of accounts the server no longer has, which took one on issue #45, the removal a person asks for over their own history, which took one on issue #46, and the register of what each account has said about being named, which took one on issue #42 and is the first spared file that writes anything: the row it writes is that account's own answer, asked for by that account. Those last five are the spared files that write no play at all, and any of them could have escaped this rule instead by naming a narrower interface or the concrete store, which is the pattern narrowed until it matches nothing rather than a rule argued with. Every line added here is a file that may reach the store without passing the gate, so the list is the cost of the rule rather than a formality: what keeps it honest is that removal is the only thing a spared file is spared for, and a spared file that started writing rows would pass this rule and be caught by nothing. The query layer is the eleventh and is the first spared file that only reads: every aggregate report is answered through it, and it is spared so that the report itself may not be, which is what issue #51's second condition asks for. The suite is spared as a directory, because a test that drives a store is what proves any of this. Issue #39, and issue #51 for the query layer.

id: no-external-host-in-a-page-script
paths: *.js
pattern: (import|export)[^;]*[[:space:]]from[[:space:]]*["'](https?:)?//|import\([[:space:]]*["'](https?:)?//|(fetch|importScripts|Worker|EventSource|WebSocket)\([[:space:]]*["'`](https?:)?//|(src|href)[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*["'`](https?:)?//
why: The rule beside this one reads tracked HTML, and a rule carries one path glob, so the moment a page keeps its script in a file of its own the guarantee stops at the page and the script is unread. This is that half. A module imported, fetched or run from another host is code nobody in this repository reviewed, running in a signed-in administrator's dashboard, and it fails outright on a server with no way out to the internet. Everything a page needs is served from the plugin, and the charts are drawn by a module of this plugin's own. Issue #62.

id: no-test-that-binds-a-port
paths: *
pattern: \b(TcpListener|TcpClient|UdpClient|HttpListener|IPEndPoint|UseKestrel|UseUrls|ListenLocalhost|ListenAnyIP)\b|System\.Net\.Sockets|new[[:space:]]+Socket\(
why: The headless policy says no test may need a listening port, and until this rule the only half of that a machine held was the privileged one, through the elevation rule. An unprivileged port asks for no rights, so it arrives quietly: the near miss takes port zero and reads back what the operating system granted, which is the shape somebody writes while avoiding a fixed port rather than while breaking a rule. What it costs is a resource shared with everything else on the runner, so two jobs on one machine race for it, a firewall asks whoever is sitting at the machine, and a listener the run failed to stop outlives the test. It buys nothing the plugin needs proved: which caller a controller admits is the same answer over a socket as over an in-memory transport, and the transport is what the endpoint tests are meant to use. The rule refuses the bind and the host that performs one, not the in-process host, so it leaves the replacement it is pointing at buildable. It reads every tracked file rather than the suite alone, like the four rules beside it, because a port is as easily opened by a service container in a workflow as by a line in a test. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #25.

id: no-time-offset-from-the-request
paths: *.cs
pattern: \[From(Query|Route|Body|Form|Header)\][^,)]*([Uu]tc[Oo]ffset|[Tt]ime[Zz]one|[Tt]imezone|[Tt]z[Oo]ffset|[Hh]our[Oo]ffset|[Oo]ffset[Mm]inutes|[Oo]ffset[Hh]ours|[Mm]inutes[Ff]rom[Uu]tc)
why: A day is not the same interval for everybody, so a report has to be told whose midnight is meant, and the closest prior art is told by the browser on every request. That number is the offset in force where the caller is sitting at the moment they asked, applied to rows recorded months earlier, so every day either side of a summer transition is shifted by an hour and the plays near midnight land on the wrong day. The rows do not move, the report does, and it moves back in October. Here the zone is named once in the settings and read at the moment a request is served, which is a rule about a place rather than a number about a moment. The pattern refuses a zone taken from the request as well as an offset, because a zone the caller chooses is the same defect with a better spelling: two people open one report and are shown different days. What it costs is that the list of spellings is closed, so a name nobody has written yet walks through it, and a paging offset is deliberately outside it, because no grep tells one offset from another. Issue #50, third condition.

id: no-zone-read-in-a-page-script
paths: *.js
pattern: getTimezoneOffset|resolvedOptions
why: The other end of the rule above. An endpoint cannot take an offset from a request unless something sends one, and what sends one is a view that read the machine the page is open on. Both calls this refuses are that reading: the offset now, in minutes, which is today's offset and not the one in force when the rows were recorded, and the zone name, which is closer to right and still the wrong source. The zone travels on the answer instead, named by the setting the figures were produced under, and a view handed figures without one refuses to draw rather than picking a boundary of its own. The rule reads tracked JavaScript, which is every page script in the tree; the two names are the whole of what a browser will tell a script about its own zone, and a view that worked one out by comparing two formatted dates is a shape no grep here can see. Issue #50, third condition, and issue #62 for why a page asset is scanned at all.

id: no-read-without-its-bound
paths: *.cs
except-in: Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests
pattern: (^|[^$])@?"[[:space:]]*(SELECT|WITH)\b
why: An endpoint that returns everything is a way to make the server do arbitrary work from one request, and it is also how a page becomes unusable on a large server. Every read here therefore says on its own first line what stops it growing with the file, and the rule refuses a statement that opens with its verb instead, which is a read whose bound nobody stated. Two shapes of mark answer that. A statement carrying its own limit says so, and the closed set of three justifications is for the statements that legitimately carry none: walked, for a sequence the caller reads a row at a time and chose to; one number, for an aggregate that answers with one value however many rows it read; and one row per account, for an answer whose size grows with the people on a server rather than with how long it has been recording, which is the only one of the three that lives outside the SQL where no reading of the statement could find it. A fourth justification is a decision rather than a mark, and it is taken on issue #56 rather than written into a statement. What this cannot do is judge the mark: a statement marked with the wrong one of the three passes, and nothing here would find it, which is what the closed set being small is worth. An interpolated statement is left to the concatenation rule beside this one rather than being refused twice, and the suite is spared as a directory, because a test that drives a store is what proves any of this and its statements are not queries this plugin issues. Issue #56, second condition.

id: no-concrete-store-outside-the-one-function-that-opens-it
paths: *.cs
except: SqlitePlayStore.cs PluginServiceRegistrator.cs
except-in: Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests
pattern: SqlitePlayStore
why: The rule above refuses the store's interface being named outside the files it spares, and its own text says a caller could escape it by naming the concrete store instead. That was measured rather than supposed: a report planted under the aggregation namespace, taking SqlitePlayStore and reading it, walked through every rule in the tree, and the reading is on issue #51. This is the other spelling. Together the two say that a report reaches the plays through the query layer or not at all, which is issue #51's second condition, and neither of them alone does. Two files are spared and the second one is the price. SqlitePlayStore.cs is the store. PluginServiceRegistrator.cs holds the one function in this plugin that opens one, which is also the one place that knows where the data folder is, and sparing it means this rule cannot see what that file does with a store. What still holds there is the rule above, which the registrator passes because it names no interface: it hands the opening function on rather than reading anything through it. A registrator that started reading rows would pass both rules and be caught by nothing, and that is what this line costs. Issue #51.
